\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces  The two new interpretability metrics. \textsc  {CFIT}\xspace  is the one clear positive for CoT-as-interpretability: the measurements a chain points at are more influential than the ones it passes over, significantly so on \textsc  {Progression}\xspace  and \textsc  {Brain-Age}\xspace  . \textsc  {ESA}\xspace  is negative on the primary task---the chains cite total tau and pTau in 100\% of cases and AT8 in 98.6\%, so citation frequency carries almost no information about which biomarker matters. An explanation that names everything explains nothing. \relax }}{10}{table.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cfit_esa}{{6}{10}{The two new interpretability metrics. \cfit is the one clear positive for CoT-as-interpretability: the measurements a chain points at are more influential than the ones it passes over, significantly so on \taskthree and \tasktwo . \esa is negative on the primary task---the chains cite total tau and pTau in 100\% of cases and AT8 in 98.6\%, so citation frequency carries almost no information about which biomarker matters. An explanation that names everything explains nothing. \relax }{table.caption.10}{}}
\newlabel{fig:cfit}{{4a}{10}{\cfit and blinded rubric\relax }{figure.caption.11}{}}
\newlabel{sub@fig:cfit}{{a}{10}{\cfit and blinded rubric\relax }{figure.caption.11}{}}
