\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces  The clinical endpoint. Normative models are fitted on cognitively normal scans only and the regression-to-the-mean bias is removed within controls, so the control gap is zero by construction. The conventional models reproduce the textbook finding of roughly four years of apparent brain ageing in dementia; the \textsc  {CoT}\xspace  -derived gap carries no disease signal at all (AUC 0.490, $p=0.670$). The chains are articulate about atrophy and ageing, and the quantity they produce is diagnostically empty. \relax }}{12}{table.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:brainage}{{8}{12}{The clinical endpoint. Normative models are fitted on cognitively normal scans only and the regression-to-the-mean bias is removed within controls, so the control gap is zero by construction. The conventional models reproduce the textbook finding of roughly four years of apparent brain ageing in dementia; the \cotarm -derived gap carries no disease signal at all (AUC 0.490, $p=0.670$). The chains are articulate about atrophy and ageing, and the quantity they produce is diagnostically empty. \relax }{table.caption.14}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces  The dissociation. The shuffled-explanation control passes---the rubric reliably separates a genuine rationale from one written for a different patient---so the scores are discriminative rather than a rubber stamp. Given that, the headline is that \textsc  {Tau-Stage}\xspace  chains score 3.67/4 for plausibility while responding to a 10$\times $ corruption of their own content 3.6\% of the time. Scores are LLM-proxy ratings, not clinician ratings. \relax }}{12}{table.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:judge}{{9}{12}{The dissociation. The shuffled-explanation control passes---the rubric reliably separates a genuine rationale from one written for a different patient---so the scores are discriminative rather than a rubber stamp. Given that, the headline is that \taskone chains score 3.67/4 for plausibility while responding to a 10$\times $ corruption of their own content 3.6\% of the time. Scores are LLM-proxy ratings, not clinician ratings. \relax }{table.caption.15}{}}
